245.m:
[The first rib belongs] to Indrāgnī; the second rib to Sarasvatī; the third to Mitra; the fourth to Soma;
the fifth to Nirṛti; the sixth to Agnīṣoma; the seventh to the serpents; the eighth to Viṣṇu;
the ninth to Pūṣan; the tenth to Tvaṣṭṛ; the eleventh to Indra; the twelfth to Varuṇa; the thirteenth to Yamī.
The right flank [belongs] to Heaven and Earth; the left to the All-Gods.

The shoulders [belong] to the Maruts; the first thoracic vertebra to the All-Gods; the second to the Rudras; the third to the Ādityas; the tail to Vāyu; the two haunches to Agnīṣoma; the two curlews to the hips; Indrābṛhaspatī to the thighs; Mitrāvaruṇā to the shanks; stepping forward to the lower legs; strength to the knees.

[We gratify] Pūṣan with the rectum; the blind-snakes with the large intestine; the serpents with the entrails; the twisted spirits with the guts; the waters with the bladder; the bull with the testicles;
the courser with the penis; offspring with the semen; the blue jays with the bile; the clefts with the anus; the kūśmas with the lumps of dung.

246.m:
the sky with the stomach; the two ruddy geese with the two kidneys; heaven with the kidneys; the mountains with the sinews;
the stones with the spleen; the ant-hills with the lungs; the clumps of grass with the glands; the flowing streams with the veins;
the ponds with the flanks; the ocean with the abdomen; Vaiśvānara with the ashes;

247.m:
the parting of the hair with the navel; ghee with the sap; waters with the broth; rays of light with the drops; hail with the rheum;
with blood, the demons; the constellations with the marks; the earth with the skin; to Jumbaka, svāhā!

Hiranyagarbha arose in the beginning;
born, he was the sole lord of what has come to be.
He supported this earth and heaven:
to what god shall we pay homage with the oblation?

Whose are these snow-clad mountains through his greatness;
whose, they say, is the ocean along with the stream Rasā;
whose are these directions, whose the arms:
to what god shall we pay homage with the oblation?

248.m:
Them we invoke with ancient invocation: Bhaga, Mitra, Aditi, infallible Dakṣa,
Aryaman, Varuṇa, Soma, the Aśvins;
may gracious Sarasvatī bring us bliss.

May the wind blow that medicine, bringing bliss;
may mother earth and father heaven do so;
so may the pressing-stones, pressers of soma, bringing bliss;
hear that, O Aśvins worthy of sacred platforms!

251.m:
Agni, be our closest friend, our protector, benevolent, a defense;
as Vasu, Agni, of good fame, approach us, give most brilliant wealth;
so to you, most flaming, shining one, we now pray for favor for our comrades.

255.m:
Make the pathways compliant with our wish;
may I be in harmony with that person,
so that I may speak this auspicious speech to the people:

258.m:
We invoke Vaiśvānara, lord of the light of truth;
we ask for the unceasing hot offering.
You are taken with a support; for Vaiśvānara [I take] you; this is your womb; for Vaiśvānara you!

261.m:
Attended by the Maruts, O Indra, bull, drink the Soma for joy, according to your custom, for exhilaration;
pour the wave of sweetness into your belly; you are the king of the freshly pressed [draughts].
You are taken with a support; for Indra attended by the Maruts [I take] you; this is your womb; for Indra attended by the Maruts you!

262.m:
May the great Indra, bolt in hand, the sixteenfold one, grant shelter;
may he slay the evil one who hates us.
You are taken with a support; for Indra the sixteenfold [I take] you; this is your womb; for Indra the sixteenfold you!

265.m:
agniśca, three [verses]; bṛhaspate, one; indra goman, one; ṛtāvānam, one; vaiśvānaram, one;
vaiśvānaraḥ, one; vaiśvānarasya sumatau, one; marutvān indra, one; mahān indra, one;
agnirṛṣiḥ, one; anuvīraiḥ, one; ā no gotrā, thus one; fourteen in twelve anuvākas.
Thus ends the twenty-eighth chapter in the Śukla Yajurveda Kāṇva Saṃhitā.

274.m:
Agni as Hotṛ has this sacrificer chosen today, cooking the cooked meats, cooking the sacrificial cake, binding the he-goat for Indra.
A good lap today has the divine Vanaspati become for Indra with the he-goat;
He has eaten from the fat; he accepted it when cooked; he has made [him] flourish with the sacrificial cake. Thee today, O seer!

289.m:
Bring [the fire-drill] down onto the supine [wood], knowing;
at once, being impregnated, she has brought forth the bull.
A reddish crest, gleaming is his front; / the son of Iḷā was born according to design.
